            <ArticleTitle>[Neuralgia of the superior laryngeal nerve caused by phonatory malfunctions].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>379-83</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Clinical observations show a close relationship between neuralgia of the superior laryngeal nerve and disorders of the larynx. Neuralgia, and more minor symptoms are usually caused by hyper- and hypotonic phonatory disorders. An unphysiological compensation for glottic insufficiency causes irritation of the sensory telodendrons of the superior laryngeal nerve. As incomplete adduction of the vocal cords can often be found in patients with an autonomic laryngeal dystonia, a syndrome related to anxiety, these disturbances are often misinterpreted as &quot;globus hystericus&quot;. However, this diagnosis does not take into account the cause and should therefore no longer be used.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
